# Cloudflare Pages equivalent of the "redirects" + "rewrites" blocks in
# vercel.json. Pages does NOT read vercel.json.
#
# Order matters — the FIRST matching rule wins, so the specific old-WordPress
# URLs must come before the catch-all at the bottom. Real files on disk are
# served before any /* rule is considered, which is what keeps /assets/*.avif,
# /videos/*.mp4, favicon.png etc. working.
#
# Limit is 2,000 static redirects; this uses 17.

# ---- old WordPress URLs Google still has indexed ----
# 301 (not 302) so the ranking transfers to the new page.
/contact-us/                                            /#contact     301
/contact-us                                             /#contact     301
/services/                                              /#treatments  301
/our-service/                                           /#treatments  301
/all-treatment/                                         /#treatments  301
/treatment/                                             /#treatments  301
/hydrafacial-treatment/                                 /#treatments  301
/pigmentation-treatment/                                /#treatments  301
/laser-treatment/                                       /#treatments  301
/laser-hair-reduction-lhr-clinic-in-delhi/              /#treatments  301
/growth-factor-concentrate-gfc-treatment/               /#treatments  301
/high-intensity-focused-ultrasound-hifu-treatment-clinic/ /#treatments 301
/kotil-expert/                                          /#tech        301
/kotil-skin-experts/                                    /#tech        301

# ---- the one real second route ----
# 200, not 301: this rewrites (serves index.html AT /consult) rather than
# redirecting, so App renders with consult=true and the URL stays put.
/consult/                                               /index.html   200
/consult                                                /index.html   200

# ---- everything else ----
# Any other path is a dead WordPress URL. 301 it to the homepage rather than
# soft-serving the site at a bogus address, which is what Google penalises.
# This replaces the big negative-lookahead regex in vercel.json: it is not
# needed here because Pages serves real files before reaching this line.
/*                                                      /             301
